[# Section 16 — capabilities: the deny-by-default permission model and
   the --allow-* grant flags. Spec home: spec/03-approved/core/security.md
   (model), spec/03-approved/misc/cli.md §3.7 (CLI flags). Every command
   and output on this page was run against the live binary (issue #425
   coverage gap #1). #]

[section id=capabilities group='Ring 1 — Code' n=16 title='Capabilities and permissions'

  [intro
    """A CX evaluation runs under an explicit **capability set**, and the
       set is empty unless you grant something: **deny-by-default, no
       ambient authority**. Pure computation — parsing, canonical emit,
       CXPath selection, in-memory transforms — needs no capability at
       all. Any operation with an external effect (reading a file,
       dialing a host, spawning a process, even reading the clock)
       checks the active set at the effect point and raises the
       capability error `CXER0271` when the matching grant is absent.
       The model is specified in `spec/03-approved/core/security.md`; this section
       is the practical guide to the `--allow-*` flags."""]

  [child id=caps-model n=16.1 title='Deny-by-default'
    [body
      """Run a program with no flags and it can compute anything but
         touch nothing. This program tries to read a file:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/io']
[$io:read-file 'VERSION']"""]
    [body
      """Without a grant, the read is refused at the effect point —
         before any filesystem access happens:"""]
    [output
      """[err code=cx-err:CXER0271 message='E_CAP_DENIED: read capability required for io-read-file; none granted (grant via --allow-read)']"""]
    [body
      """The error is **actionable by contract**: it names the missing
         capability, the operation that wanted it, and the exact flag
         that grants it. Add the grant and the same program works:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx --allow-read read_version.cx
'0.16.0\\n'"""]
    [body
      """Grants combine — a program that reads the environment and
         writes a file needs both flags:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx --allow-env --allow-write snapshot_home.cx"""]
    [note
      """A denied effect is refused **fail-closed, before any type or
         domain validation** — the path is never touched, the socket
         never opened. And because the check happens at the effect
         point, a `pure` function can never raise `CXER0271` under any
         capability set: purity is capability-freedom by construction
         (the effect-totality rule in `spec/03-approved/core/security.md`)."""]]

  [child id=caps-grants n=16.2 title='The grant list'
    [body
      """Nine capabilities cover every effectful surface in the engine
         and standard library, plus two broad grants. This is the
         full set the binary accepts — there are no hidden grants, and
         a misspelled grant flag (`--alow-net`, `--allow-nett`, …) is a
         hard usage error (exit 2) naming the flag, never a silent
         no-grant:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'flag'] [th 'capability'] [th 'gates']]
      [tr [td '--allow-read'] [td 'read'] [td 'filesystem reads, [?cx include], terminal input']]
      [tr [td '--allow-write'] [td 'write'] [td 'filesystem writes']]
      [tr [td '--allow-net[=host[:port]]'] [td 'net'] [td 'HTTP client and server, sockets, network stores; see the scoping subsection']]
      [tr [td '--allow-env'] [td 'env'] [td 'environment-variable reads']]
      [tr [td '--allow-clock'] [td 'clock'] [td 'wall-clock reads, profiling timers, non-mock [?sleep]']]
      [tr [td '--allow-random'] [td 'random'] [td 'CSPRNG / OS entropy — crypto, uuid, random']]
      [tr [td '--allow-subprocess'] [td 'subprocess'] [td 'process spawn ([$process:run] and friends)']]
      [tr [td '--allow-eval'] [td 'eval'] [td 'dynamic evaluation — [?eval] tree-eval']]
      [tr [td '--allow-secret-reveal'] [td 'secret-reveal'] [td 'declassifying a [?secret] value via [?reveal]']]
      [tr [td '--allow-common'] [td '(all but secret-reveal)'] [td 'the common working set — everything above except declassifying secrets']]
      [tr [td '--allow-all'] [td '(all)'] [td 'every capability — explicit trusted-local opt-out']]]
    [body
      """Each denial names its own grant. A few worked examples, each
         run live. Reading the environment:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/env']
[$env:var 'HOME']"""]
    [output
      """$ cx home.cx
[err code=cx-err:CXER0271 message='E_CAP_DENIED: env capability required for env-var; none granted (grant via --allow-env)']
$ cx --allow-env home.cx
'/Users/ep'"""]
    [body """Reading the clock:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/time']
[$time:now]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-clock now.cx
'2026-07-14T22:11:55.071838Z'"""]
    [body """Asking for entropy:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/uuid']
[$uuid:v4]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-random id.cx
'2c9be10f-2a88-456f-b00d-9af606793775'"""]
    [body """Spawning a subprocess:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/process']
[$process:run ('echo', 'hi')]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-subprocess run_echo.cx
[proc-result exit-code=0 signaled=false timed-out=false stdout='hi
' stderr='']"""]
    [body
      """Evaluating a constructed tree — dynamic evaluation is a
         capability like any other, so a program that builds and runs
         code at runtime says so on the command line:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?eval [?quote [+ $a $b]] [context {a: 10, b: 5}]]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-eval calc.cx
15"""]
    [body
      """Secrets are the inverse case: **wrapping** a value with
         [?secret] needs no grant, and the wrapped value renders
         redacted everywhere — logs, output, serialization:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?let [= $token [?secret 'hunter2']]
  $token]"""]
    [output
      """'‹redacted›'"""]
    [body
      """Only **declassifying** it is gated: `[?reveal $token]` raises
         `CXER0271` unless the program runs with
         `--allow-secret-reveal`."""]
    [note
      """**`--allow-net` is the only grant that scopes.** A resource
         suffix on `--allow-read`, `--allow-write` or `--allow-env` is
         now a **usage error** — `cx --allow-read=./data prog.cx`
         refuses at startup with exit 2, before the program is
         evaluated, and names the flag, the suffix it would have
         ignored, and the bare spelling that is accepted. Earlier
         versions accepted the suffix, discarded it, and granted the
         **blanket** capability, so a caller who typed the narrower
         form silently got the wider authority; refusing is the
         fail-closed answer until scoping actually exists. Write
         `--allow-read` and mean it, or scope the one grant that can
         be scoped."""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx --allow-read=./data prog.cx"""]
    [output
      """cx: --allow-read=./data: read scoping is NOT IMPLEMENTED — the resource `./data` would be IGNORED and the grant would be BLANKET read authority over the whole filesystem, not the narrowing this spells (cx-home/cx-private#1059)
  the accepted spelling is the bare grant:  --allow-read
  it grants read over the whole filesystem — which is what the suffixed form has always done — so say it explicitly instead of appearing to narrow it
  real per-path read scoping is tracked at cx-home/cx-private#1061
  `--allow-net=host[:port]` is the one grant whose scope IS enforced today"""]
    [body
      """Per-path and per-name enforcement is specified
         (`spec/03-approved/core/security.md`, coarse-v1 scoping) and
         lands per domain. The remaining capabilities — `clock`,
         `random`, `subprocess`, `eval`, `secret-reveal` — still accept
         a suffix and still ignore it; do not read one as a
         boundary."""]]

  [child id=caps-net n=16.3 title='Scoping the network grant'
    [body
      """`--allow-net` is the one grant with enforced resource scoping,
         because it is the one most worth narrowing. Three levels:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'flag'] [th 'meaning']]
      [tr [td '(none)'] [td 'every dial and bind raises CXER0271 naming the host:port it wanted']]
      [tr [td '--allow-net'] [td 'any public host — but private, loopback, and link-local ranges stay denied (CXER4504)']]
      [tr [td '--allow-net=HOST[:PORT]'] [td 'least privilege: only the named host (and port) is dialable; a literal-IP or localhost scope also admits that private address']]]
    [body
      """The private-range deny set on the bare grant is deliberate:
         `--allow-net` means "talk to the internet", not "probe my
         loopback and LAN". A program handed a URL that resolves into
         `127.0.0.0/8`, `10.0.0.0/8`, `192.168.0.0/16`, and the other
         reserved ranges gets a refusal:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/http']
[$http:get 'http://127.0.0.1:1/health']"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-net probe.cx
[err code=cx-err:CXER4504 message='E_NET_FORBIDDEN_ADDRESS: 127.0.0.1:1 resolves into a denied range with no admitting literal-IP/localhost grant (§4.5)']"""]
    [body
      """To talk to a local service, name it — a literal-IP or
         `localhost` scope overrides the deny set for exactly that
         address:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx --allow-net=127.0.0.1:8443 probe.cx"""]
    [body
      """And a host-scoped grant is a real boundary: with
         `--allow-net=api.example.com:443`, a dial to any other host is
         refused with a `CXER0271` naming the host and port it
         wanted — so the denial tells you exactly what to review before
         widening the grant."""]]

  [child id=caps-broad-grants n=16.4 title='The two broad grants'
    [body
      """Enumerating capabilities per script gets tiresome while you
         are still exploring, so there are two broad grants. They differ
         in exactly one capability, and that one matters."""]
    [body
      """`--allow-common` grants the common working set: read, write,
         net, env, clock, random, subprocess, eval. It does **not**
         grant `secret-reveal`, so a `[?reveal]` under it still refuses
         with `CXER0271` — and the denial propagates out of the `[out]`
         wrapper to the top level, printed as a **bare** `[err …]`
         value with exit code 1. This is the grant to reach for while
         you are learning, and the one these pages use."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?let [= $s [?secret 'hunter2']]
  [out [?reveal $s]]]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-common reveal.cx
[err code=cx-err:CXER0271 message='E_CAP_DENIED: secret-reveal capability required for reveal; none granted (grant via --allow-secret-reveal)']
$ cx --allow-all reveal.cx
[out 'hunter2']"""]
    [body
      """`--allow-all` grants everything, `secret-reveal` included. It
         exists because deny-by-default must stay ergonomic for
         fully-trusted local use — your own build script, a REPL
         experiment, a one-off transform on your own machine. It is an
         explicit, visible opt-out: anyone reading the invocation can
         see the program runs unconfined."""]
    [body
      """Where **not** to use either: anything long-running, anything
         network-facing, anything whose inputs you do not control, and
         any invocation you copy into documentation, CI, a systemd
         unit, or a Dockerfile. Those should carry the narrowest set
         that works — the flags are the audit trail. Note that
         `--allow-all` is strictly wider than granting all nine flags
         individually, `--allow-common` included: it is the only grant
         that bypasses the private-range deny set on network dials, so
         a local-service example under `--allow-common` asks for the
         scope it needs (`--allow-net=127.0.0.1:PORT`)."""]]

  [child id=caps-narrowing n=16.5 title='Narrowing with [?with-caps]'
    [body
      """A program can **narrow** its own set for a dynamic extent —
         never widen it. `[?with-caps]` takes one or more
         `[deny CAP]` clauses and a body; inside the body the denied
         capabilities are gone, even if the process was launched with
         them, and they come back when the body exits:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/env']
[?with-caps [deny env]
  [$env:var 'HOME']]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-env narrowed.cx
[err code=cx-err:CXER0271 message='E_CAP_DENIED: env capability required for env-var; none granted (grant via --allow-env)']"""]
    [body
      """This is the tool for running untrusted sub-computations:
         evaluate a tree you did not author under
         `[?with-caps [deny net] [deny subprocess] …]` and its
         authority is bounded no matter what it contains. A `deny`
         cannot be undone inside the body — narrowing is
         one-way by design."""]]

  [child id=caps-errors n=16.6 title='Denials are values'
    [body
      """`CXER0271` is a normal error **value**, not a crash. It flows
         through the program like any other value, so a program can
         detect a missing grant and degrade gracefully with
         `[?match]` (or `[?else]` / `[?fallback]`):"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/io']
[?match [$io:read-file 'config.cx']
  [case [err $e] 'no read grant or no config - using defaults']
  [else 'loaded config']]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx degrade.cx
'no read grant or no config - using defaults'"""]
    [body
      """What a denial can never do is succeed silently: there is no
         fallback value, no partial effect, no environment where the
         denied operation "sort of" ran. Either the grant is present
         and the effect happens, or you hold an `[err]` naming the
         grant."""]
    [note
      """This guide eats the same dog food: every `cx` example on these
         pages is executed against the live binary by the docs-example
         gate, which grants **no** capabilities — a snippet whose only
         output is a `CXER0271` denial passes (the denial is
         environmental, not staleness), and anything else that errors
         fails the build."""]]

  [child id=security n=16.7 title='Untrusted inputs and untrusted code'
    [intro
      """The capability model is one half of CX's security posture; this
         subsection is the whole posture in one place. The normative
         reference is the threat model
         (`spec/03-approved/process/threat-model.md`), which assumes one
         of three deployment shapes: **A — trusted inputs**
         (operator-authored files; defaults suffice), **B —
         semi-trusted** (PRs, CI artifacts, third-party config; pin the
         toolchain and validate at the boundary), and **C — untrusted**
         (network payloads, public uploads; everything below, plus
         process isolation)."""]

    [body
      """CX distinguishes two threats that get conflated elsewhere:"""]
    [list
      [item """**Untrusted data, trusted code.** Your program parses
              attacker-supplied bytes. The parser is the attack surface;
              the mitigations are structural: parsing needs no
              capabilities at all, every regex call site routes through
              the vendored RE2 engine (**linear-time matching** — no
              catastrophic backtracking class, in the schema `[pattern]`
              constraint included), invalid UTF-8 is a typed refusal
              rather than a silent substitution, and a `.cxs` schema at
              the boundary type-checks every field before anything
              downstream sees the value ([[schema-evolution]])."""]
      [item """**Untrusted code.** You evaluate a tree you did not
              author. The threat is reach, and reach is exactly what the
              capability set bounds: with no grants the code can
              compute, transform, hash, validate, and project — and
              cannot read a file, dial a host, spawn a process, read the
              clock or the environment, or declassify a secret. There is
              no ambient escape hatch to close because there is no
              ambient authority to begin with."""]]

    [body
      """The idiom for running untrusted sub-computations inside a
         granted process is **narrowing**: evaluate the foreign tree
         under `[?with-caps [deny …] …]` (see the narrowing subsection
         above) and its authority is bounded no matter what it contains
         — a `deny` cannot be undone from inside. Dynamic evaluation is
         itself a capability (`--allow-eval`), so a program that runs
         runtime-supplied source says so on its command line, visibly."""]

    [body
      """What the capability model does **not** bound: total CPU and
         memory spent inside pure computation. A hostile-but-pure
         program can still burn cycles, so deployment shape C pairs the
         empty capability set with **OS-level process isolation** —
         memory caps, wall-clock timeouts, file-descriptor limits — and
         monitoring on the refusal codes. Output of untrusted evaluation
         is a value, not sanitized markup: HTML contexts route it
         through an escaping renderer, never string concatenation."""]

    [note
      """CX has not yet received an external security audit. The
         hardening above is real and continuously exercised (the
         conformance corpus, the capability-denial gates, reproducible
         builds), but until an audit lands, the recommended posture for
         shape C is the belt-and-suspenders one: empty grant set,
         narrowing around foreign trees, schema validation at the
         boundary, and process isolation around the whole thing."""]]
]
